\chapter{Choosing a grammar engine}
MuDSL offers three distinct parsing tools. Whole-input patterns recognize
small character languages. Parser combinators construct typed parsers from
smaller parsers. Explicit context-free grammars use Earley parsing and can
represent left-recursive and nullable productions directly.

\section{Match the engine to the problem}
Use \api{pattern} for small validation rules, such as a numeric identifier.
Use \api{StaticRegex} when byte-oriented grouping, alternation, and compiled
regular-expression structure matter. Both differ from a PEG rule, which
recognizes a prefix and participates in ordered parsing.

Combinators are convenient when the grammar is part of the host program and
semantic result types are known in advance. The \api{try_parse} operation
explicitly restores backtracking after a committed failure. Repetition must
reject a parser that succeeds without consuming input; otherwise an innocent
empty production becomes an infinite loop.

An explicit CFG is appropriate when grammar data is itself supplied at runtime.
Its terminals are tokens chosen by your lexer. Earley parsing recognizes left
recursion without rewriting the grammar into a precedence ladder, but an
ambiguous grammar still requires a policy for choosing a derivation.

\section{Precedence parsing}
MuLL uses a compact precedence parser because its operator table is fixed and
small. This gives a direct correspondence between the reference table in
Chapter 51 and the implementation. The reusable toolkit remains available for
other languages with different grammar requirements.
